---
/**
 * Property Ownership Options Page
 *
 * This page presents 5 MECE (Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive) options
 * for tenants who want to take ownership of a property currently owned by a Town.
 *
 * MECE Principle:
 * - Mutually Exclusive: Each option represents a distinct acquisition pathway
 *   that does not overlap with the others.
 * - Collectively Exhaustive: Together, the 5 options cover every reasonable
 *   avenue for a tenant to acquire ownership — from private negotiation,
 *   legal tenant rights, structured financing, collective community action,
 *   to public policy / government transfer programs.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Property Ownership Options — 5 MECE Recommendations">

    <!-- Page Title & Introduction -->
    <section class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="mb-6">Property Ownership Options</h1>
        <p class="text-lg mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            As tenants in a property owned by the Town, there are several distinct
            pathways to explore for taking over ownership. The following
            <strong>5 recommendations</strong> are structured using the
            <strong>MECE principle</strong> (Mutually Exclusive, Collectively
            Exhaustive) — meaning each option is a separate, non-overlapping
            strategy, and together they cover all viable approaches.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm opacity-75">
            Each option includes a description, key steps, advantages, risks,
            and an estimated timeline to help guide decision-making.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- OPTION 1: Direct Purchase at Market Value                    -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-14 p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content font-bold text-lg">1</span>
            <h2 class="!mt-0 !mb-0">Direct Purchase at Market Value</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Description of what this option involves -->
        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            Negotiate a straightforward sale with the Town Council or municipal
            authority. The property is appraised at fair market value by an
            independent valuer, and you (the tenant) purchase it outright through
            a standard real-estate transaction. This is the most conventional and
            fastest route if financing is available.
        </p>

        <!-- Key steps the tenant would need to follow -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Key Steps</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 mb-4 ml-2">
            <li>Submit a formal Letter of Interest to the Town's property disposal office.</li>
            <li>Request an independent market valuation of the property.</li>
            <li>Secure financing (mortgage, cash reserves, or bridging loan).</li>
            <li>Negotiate sale terms, including price, settlement date, and conditions.</li>
            <li>Execute the Sale and Purchase Agreement and complete legal transfer.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Advantages and risks laid out side by side -->
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Advantages</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Simplest and most transparent process.</li>
                    <li>Full ownership rights from day one of settlement.</li>
                    <li>No dependency on legislative or policy changes.</li>
                    <li>Clear and predictable timeline.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-red-400">Risks</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Market-value price may be prohibitively high.</li>
                    <li>Town may not be willing to sell voluntarily.</li>
                    <li>Competing offers from third parties could drive up price.</li>
                    <li>Requires upfront capital or loan eligibility.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Estimated timeline for this option -->
        <p class="text-sm opacity-75">
            <strong>Estimated Timeline:</strong> 3 – 9 months (subject to Town
            Council approval cycles and financing arrangements).
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- OPTION 2: Tenant's Right of First Refusal / Pre-emptive Right -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-14 p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content font-bold text-lg">2</span>
            <h2 class="!mt-0 !mb-0">Tenant's Right of First Refusal / Pre-emptive Right</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Description of what this option involves -->
        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            Investigate and exercise any legal or contractual right you may hold
            as an existing tenant to purchase the property before it is offered to
            outside buyers. Many jurisdictions grant sitting tenants a statutory
            "Right of First Refusal" (ROFR) when a public landlord decides to
            dispose of a property. Even where no statute exists, your lease
            agreement may contain such a clause.
        </p>

        <!-- Key steps the tenant would need to follow -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Key Steps</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 mb-4 ml-2">
            <li>Review your lease agreement for any purchase option or ROFR clause.</li>
            <li>Research local/state legislation on tenant pre-emptive purchase rights.</li>
            <li>Engage a property lawyer to confirm legal standing and enforce the right.</li>
            <li>Formally notify the Town in writing that you wish to exercise the right.</li>
            <li>Negotiate and close the purchase on the terms prescribed by law or contract.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Advantages and risks laid out side by side -->
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Advantages</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>May secure the property at a fixed or below-market price.</li>
                    <li>Legal protection against being outbid by third parties.</li>
                    <li>Reinforces your position as a long-standing tenant.</li>
                    <li>Often supported by tenant-protection legislation.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-red-400">Risks</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Right may not exist in your jurisdiction or lease.</li>
                    <li>Strict procedural requirements; missing deadlines forfeits the right.</li>
                    <li>Town may challenge the applicability of the right.</li>
                    <li>Legal costs if enforcement goes to court.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Estimated timeline for this option -->
        <p class="text-sm opacity-75">
            <strong>Estimated Timeline:</strong> 2 – 12 months (depending on
            whether the right is clearly established or needs litigation).
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- OPTION 3: Lease-to-Own (Rent-to-Buy) Agreement               -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-14 p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content font-bold text-lg">3</span>
            <h2 class="!mt-0 !mb-0">Lease-to-Own (Rent-to-Buy) Agreement</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Description of what this option involves -->
        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            Propose converting the existing tenancy into a structured
            lease-to-own arrangement where a portion of each rental payment is
            credited toward the eventual purchase price. At the end of the agreed
            term, you acquire full ownership. This option is ideal when upfront
            capital is limited but the Town is willing to facilitate a gradual
            transfer of ownership.
        </p>

        <!-- Key steps the tenant would need to follow -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Key Steps</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 mb-4 ml-2">
            <li>Propose the lease-to-own concept formally to the Town Council.</li>
            <li>Agree on the total purchase price, lease term, and rent-credit percentage.</li>
            <li>Engage solicitors to draft a Lease-Option or Lease-Purchase agreement.</li>
            <li>Make regular payments, with an agreed portion credited to equity.</li>
            <li>Exercise the purchase option at the end of the lease term to complete transfer.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Advantages and risks laid out side by side -->
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Advantages</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Low or no upfront capital requirement.</li>
                    <li>Builds equity gradually while continuing to occupy the property.</li>
                    <li>Price is locked in at the start, protecting against appreciation.</li>
                    <li>Demonstrates commitment, which may appeal to the Town Council.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-red-400">Risks</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Town may not agree to this unconventional arrangement.</li>
                    <li>Long commitment period (typically 3–10 years).</li>
                    <li>Risk of losing accumulated credits if you default on payments.</li>
                    <li>Property maintenance responsibilities may shift to you during the lease.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Estimated timeline for this option -->
        <p class="text-sm opacity-75">
            <strong>Estimated Timeline:</strong> 3 – 10 years (the lease-to-own
            period), with the initial agreement negotiated within 3 – 6 months.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- OPTION 4: Community / Cooperative Acquisition                -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-14 p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content font-bold text-lg">4</span>
            <h2 class="!mt-0 !mb-0">Community / Cooperative Acquisition</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Description of what this option involves -->
        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            Form a community group, cooperative, or tenant association to
            collectively acquire the property from the Town. By pooling financial
            resources and presenting a community-benefit case, the group can often
            access preferential pricing, grants, or community development funding
            that individual buyers cannot. Ownership is held collectively, with
            each member holding a share or unit.
        </p>

        <!-- Key steps the tenant would need to follow -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Key Steps</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 mb-4 ml-2">
            <li>Organise fellow tenants or community members into a formal entity (co-op, trust, or association).</li>
            <li>Develop a community-benefit business plan and governance structure.</li>
            <li>Apply for community development grants, social investment, or cooperative financing.</li>
            <li>Present the collective proposal to the Town with evidence of community support.</li>
            <li>Negotiate and execute the transfer of ownership to the community entity.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Advantages and risks laid out side by side -->
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Advantages</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Shared financial burden reduces individual cost.</li>
                    <li>Access to grants and community-specific funding.</li>
                    <li>Strong political goodwill — Town Councils often favor community ownership.</li>
                    <li>Long-term stability through collective governance.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-red-400">Risks</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Requires significant coordination and consensus among members.</li>
                    <li>Complex legal and governance structure to set up.</li>
                    <li>Individual ownership is diluted (you hold a share, not sole title).</li>
                    <li>Grant applications can be lengthy and competitive.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Estimated timeline for this option -->
        <p class="text-sm opacity-75">
            <strong>Estimated Timeline:</strong> 6 – 24 months (including entity
            formation, funding applications, and negotiation with the Town).
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- OPTION 5: Government Transfer / Municipal Disposition Program -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-14 p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content font-bold text-lg">5</span>
            <h2 class="!mt-0 !mb-0">Government Transfer / Municipal Disposition Program</h2>
        </div>

        <!-- Description of what this option involves -->
        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
            Pursue acquisition through an existing (or proposed) government
            program designed for the disposal or transfer of publicly-owned
            property. Many municipalities have surplus-property programs,
            homesteading initiatives, or urban renewal schemes that allow
            qualifying tenants or residents to acquire public properties at
            below-market rates or even for a nominal fee. If no such program
            exists, lobbying for a policy change or council resolution is part of
            this pathway.
        </p>

        <!-- Key steps the tenant would need to follow -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Key Steps</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 mb-4 ml-2">
            <li>Research existing municipal property disposal or surplus-property programs.</li>
            <li>Determine eligibility criteria and application requirements.</li>
            <li>If no program exists, petition the Town Council or lobby elected representatives to create one.</li>
            <li>Submit a formal application or proposal under the relevant program.</li>
            <li>Complete any required conditions (e.g., property improvement commitments) and finalize the transfer.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- Advantages and risks laid out side by side -->
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Advantages</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Potentially the lowest-cost route (below market or nominal price).</li>
                    <li>Government programs often include support services and subsidies.</li>
                    <li>Aligns with public policy goals (housing, urban renewal).</li>
                    <li>Creates a precedent that benefits other tenants in similar situations.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 text-red-400">Risks</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-2">
                    <li>Programs may not exist or may have restrictive eligibility criteria.</li>
                    <li>Lobbying for new policy is time-consuming and uncertain.</li>
                    <li>Bureaucratic processes can cause significant delays.</li>
                    <li>Transfer may come with binding conditions (e.g., resale restrictions).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Estimated timeline for this option -->
        <p class="text-sm opacity-75">
            <strong>Estimated Timeline:</strong> 6 – 36 months (highly variable;
            faster if an existing program applies, much longer if policy advocacy
            is required).
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- MECE Summary / Comparison Table                              -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="mb-6">Summary Comparison</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/20">
                        <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">#</th>
                        <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Option</th>
                        <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Cost Level</th>
                        <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Complexity</th>
                        <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Timeline</th>
                        <th class="py-3 font-semibold">Best When…</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <!-- Row 1: Direct Purchase -->
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">1</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Direct Purchase</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">High</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Low</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">3 – 9 mo</td>
                        <td class="py-3">You have financing and the Town is willing to sell</td>
                    </tr>
                    <!-- Row 2: Right of First Refusal -->
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">2</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Right of First Refusal</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Medium</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Medium</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">2 – 12 mo</td>
                        <td class="py-3">Legal rights exist in your lease or local law</td>
                    </tr>
                    <!-- Row 3: Lease-to-Own -->
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">3</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Lease-to-Own</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Medium</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Medium</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">3 – 10 yr</td>
                        <td class="py-3">Upfront capital is limited but steady payments are feasible</td>
                    </tr>
                    <!-- Row 4: Community / Cooperative -->
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">4</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Community / Co-op</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Low – Medium</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">High</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">6 – 24 mo</td>
                        <td class="py-3">Multiple tenants can pool resources and community grants apply</td>
                    </tr>
                    <!-- Row 5: Government Transfer -->
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">5</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Government Transfer</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">Low</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">High</td>
                        <td class="py-3 pr-4">6 – 36 mo</td>
                        <td class="py-3">A municipal disposition or surplus-property program exists</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Next Steps / Call to Action                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section class="mb-12 p-6 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
        <h2 class="mb-4">Recommended Next Steps</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 ml-2 leading-relaxed">
            <li><strong>Legal Review:</strong> Engage a property lawyer to review your lease and identify any existing rights (Option 2).</li>
            <li><strong>Financial Assessment:</strong> Determine your available capital and borrowing capacity to narrow down which options are financially viable (Options 1, 3).</li>
            <li><strong>Community Consultation:</strong> Speak with fellow tenants to gauge interest in a collective approach (Option 4).</li>
            <li><strong>Municipal Research:</strong> Contact the Town's property office to inquire about disposal programs and willingness to sell (Options 1, 5).</li>
            <li><strong>Parallel Pursuit:</strong> Options are not sequential — pursue multiple avenues simultaneously to maximize your chances of success.</li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Back to home link -->
    <p class="mb-8">
        <a href="/" class="btn btn-lg sm:min-w-64">Back to Home</a>
    </p>
</Layout>
